// Which rule was broken.  A well-formed record whose CONTENT was wrong.
type ViolationSite int32

const (
	ViolationSite_VIOLATION_SITE_UNSPECIFIED              ViolationSite = 0
	ViolationSite_VIOLATION_SITE_EPOCH_MISMATCH           ViolationSite = 1 // a record under an uninstalled epoch
	ViolationSite_VIOLATION_SITE_INTERACTION_MISMATCH     ViolationSite = 2 // a replay whose bytes changed
	ViolationSite_VIOLATION_SITE_MISSING_ARRANGEMENT      ViolationSite = 3 // references something unacknowledged
	ViolationSite_VIOLATION_SITE_ZERO_ID                  ViolationSite = 4 // an identity field that must not be 0
	ViolationSite_VIOLATION_SITE_MISS_WITH_PICKED         ViolationSite = 5
	ViolationSite_VIOLATION_SITE_UNKNOWN_TOOL             ViolationSite = 6
	ViolationSite_VIOLATION_SITE_OWNED_REGION_HINT        ViolationSite = 7 // a D bit naming an Owned region
	ViolationSite_VIOLATION_SITE_IMPOSSIBLE_STATE_WORD    ViolationSite = 8
	ViolationSite_VIOLATION_SITE_INVALID_PUBLISHED_LENGTH ViolationSite = 9
	ViolationSite_VIOLATION_SITE_RELEASE_CAS_FAILED       ViolationSite = 10 // proven interference
	ViolationSite_VIOLATION_SITE_WRONG_PRODUCER_RELEASE   ViolationSite = 11 // a release naming the wrong side
	// Five rules these documents state normatively and this enum could not name, so a
	// peer that detected one had to drop the record silently or report the wrong
	// rule (`shared_memory_implementation_details.md` §2, "The `where` values").
	ViolationSite_VIOLATION_SITE_UNKNOWN_RELEASE    ViolationSite = 12 // a release naming no reservation
	ViolationSite_VIOLATION_SITE_SEQ_ON_OPEN        ViolationSite = 13 // seq != 0 on an opening record
	ViolationSite_VIOLATION_SITE_POSITION_TAIL      ViolationSite = 14 // a non-zero tail on a position payload
	ViolationSite_VIOLATION_SITE_NONFINITE_POSITION ViolationSite = 15
	ViolationSite_VIOLATION_SITE_INVALID_TARGET     ViolationSite = 16
	// Two more.  They append AFTER those above because SDKs already implement
	// those numbers; a renumber there would break peers that are already
	// written, and this enum is switched on by the host.
	//
	// An InstallSegment riding an ExportChunk that carries `inline`, which names
	// no segment for it to install into (control_plane_protocol.md §8.12).
	ViolationSite_VIOLATION_SITE_INSTALL_WITHOUT_SEGMENT ViolationSite = 17
	// A release whose `offset` does not fall inside the `region` it names -- the
	// cross-check that redundancy exists for (control_plane_protocol.md §8.12).
	// NOT `unknown_release`: that one names no reservation at all, while this one
	// names a real reservation through a region that cannot contain it.
	ViolationSite_VIOLATION_SITE_RELEASE_REGION_MISMATCH ViolationSite = 18
	// An arrangement re-sent under the same identity carrying DIFFERENT content
	// (control_plane_protocol.md §5.5 rule 4, and §11's list of host bugs a
	// plugin reports).  Its OWN site rather than `interaction_mismatch`, which
	// two SDKs reached for: that one is an E-record replay whose bytes changed,
	// and §12 aggregates per `where` under a budget a change of `where` does not
	// reset -- so sharing a code would merge two unrelated defects into one
	// counter and one retained exemplar, and point a developer at the fast path
	// for a bug in the arrangement.  `epoch_mismatch` is further still: that is a
	// RECORD under an uninstalled epoch, not an arrangement at all.
	//
	// `record` is absent here.  An arrangement is a control-plane message, not a
	// 112-byte doorbell record, which is what "when there was one" admits.
	ViolationSite_VIOLATION_SITE_ARRANGEMENT_CONTENT_MISMATCH ViolationSite = 19
	// An arrangement whose IDENTITY went BACKWARDS -- an `EpochChange` older than
	// the one installed, a `ToolBinding` whose revision regresses.  Its own site
	// rather than folded into the one above, for that one's own reason: §12
	// aggregates per `where`, so a shared code merges two defects a developer
	// wants to tell apart.  The content-mismatch case is one identity carrying
	// DIFFERENT content; this is the identity itself moving the wrong way, which
	// a receiver refuses instead of installing.
	ViolationSite_VIOLATION_SITE_ARRANGEMENT_REGRESSED ViolationSite = 20
	// A repeated field over §9's bound.  `control_plane_protocol.md` §13 asks for
	// it "refused rather than decoded", and a protobuf receiver cannot honor
	// that literally -- decoding is what produces the count.  So the obligation is
	// to refuse to APPLY it and to report under this site; both SDKs already
	// refuse to apply and kept a local diagnostic for want of a name.
	ViolationSite_VIOLATION_SITE_REPEATED_FIELD_OVERFLOW ViolationSite = 21
	// A live Pulsed overlay payload whose geometry names a SEGMENT rather than
	// carrying it inline (shared_memory_protocol.md §3.3).  A Pulsed value is
	// consumed at a moment neither end chooses, so a descriptor inside one names
	// a loan the sender cannot keep alive until the read happens: the update is
	// not applied.  Distinct from `invalid_published_length`, which is about how
	// MANY bytes a slot claims rather than what they refer to.
	ViolationSite_VIOLATION_SITE_LIVE_GEOMETRY_NOT_INLINE ViolationSite = 22
	// A live overlay payload whose `overlay_id` disagrees with the segment's
	// `subject` (§3.3).  The redundancy is the check, exactly as `region` beside
	// `offset` is on a release -- so the disagreement is reported and the update
	// is not applied, rather than one of the two being believed.
	ViolationSite_VIOLATION_SITE_LANE_SUBJECT_MISMATCH ViolationSite = 23
	// Three rules asked for a §11 report and named no site to make it with, each
	// found by two SDKs independently keeping a local diagnostic for want of a
	// name -- which is the same evidence that earned 19-23 their numbers.
	//
	// `shared_memory_protocol.md` §6.3 is the test they were measured against,
	// and it is a real test rather than a precedent for silence: revision
	// exhaustion has no site BECAUSE the ceiling is 2^62 publish attempts, so the
	// condition is structural, unreachable and unrecoverable and a value for it
	// could never be sent.  All three below are operational, reachable, and
	// fixable by a host that is told -- so they are the other answer.
	//
	// Two counts declared for one reservation (control_plane_protocol.md §5.6).
	// The receiver applies the count it was FIRST given and reports; it cannot
	// adopt the larger, because doing so accepts that the sender does not know
	// its own set, which is the one thing `uses` exists to assert.  NOT
	// `unknown_release` or `release_region_mismatch`: those are about WHICH
	// bytes, this is about how many uses were declared for them.
	ViolationSite_VIOLATION_SITE_USES_DISAGREEMENT ViolationSite = 24
	// A `Shutdown` carrying no `deadline_ms` (§9).  The receiver adopts the
	// table's 5 s rather than stalling, and reports -- waiting is the reading
	// that hangs against a non-conforming peer.  Only detectable because the
	// field is `optional`; see the comment on it.
	ViolationSite_VIOLATION_SITE_MISSING_DEADLINE ViolationSite = 25
	// An identity's FIRST arrangement carrying a revision other than 1 (§5.5
	// rule 4) -- a sender allocating from a per-session counter, so handle 7's
	// first statement is revision 12.  The receiver INSTALLS it and reports;
	// refusing costs a session over a numbering choice, since the host re-sends
	// the same revision and §9's budget escalates against a peer whose only
	// fault is arithmetic.  Its own site rather than `arrangement_regressed`
	// (an identity moving the wrong way) or `arrangement_content_mismatch` (one
	// identity carrying two contents): this identity is starting in the wrong
	// PLACE, and §12 aggregates per `where`.
	ViolationSite_VIOLATION_SITE_ARRANGEMENT_FIRST_REVISION ViolationSite = 26
	// An accepted `E` terminal whose CONTEXT cannot be reconciled with arranged
	// state (doorbell_E_input.md §3's third terminal row): a `level` the epoch's
	// `Geometry.levels` does not declare, a non-MISS voxel-naming coordinate
	// outside the clip box at that level, or a `t` past an arranged time
	// mapping's table.  The transport cannot check it (it holds no arranged
	// state) and the supervisor may not reject a terminal over it, so the
	// record is surfaced, the interaction closes, the part is marked unusable
	// and THIS is reported.
	ViolationSite_VIOLATION_SITE_UNUSABLE_CONTEXT ViolationSite = 27
	// An overlay push the host did not apply: an item the wire vocabulary
	// refuses (a bad shape, an unknown frame, explicit mesh vertices), a
	// label volume that is not a VOXEL SELECTION (`OverlayLabelVolume`),
	// or an inline field over §9's 8 MiB.
	// `detail` names the overlay, the item and the rule; the WHOLE push is not
	// applied, because an `OverlayUpdate` is a full replacement and applying
	// part of one shows a state the plugin never sent.  Host -> plugin only.
	// Before it existed a refused push was a host-side log line the plugin
	// author could not see (control_plane_protocol.md §8.5).
	ViolationSite_VIOLATION_SITE_OVERLAY_ITEM_REFUSED ViolationSite = 28
	// A panel patch OP the host did not apply: a panel or widget the manifest
	// does not declare, an image ref off the panel segment or unreadable, a PNG
	// that did not decode, an op the widget's declared kind does not take, an
	// inline field over §9's bound.  Per OP -- each op is its own statement
	// about one widget (§5.4), so its neighbours in the same patch still apply.
	// Its own site rather than 28 because §12 aggregates per `where`, and a
	// panel defect and an overlay defect are two counters a developer wants
	// apart.  Host -> plugin only.  It is how the plugin learns why nothing
	// changed (control_plane_protocol.md §8.4).
	ViolationSite_VIOLATION_SITE_PANEL_PATCH_REFUSED ViolationSite = 29
	// A chunked-overlay message the host did not apply (§5.7, §8.5): an
	// `OverlayChunk` or `OverlayLabelTable` naming an overlay the manifest does
	// not declare CHUNKED, a chunk at a level the open dataset does not have,
	// a box outside that level's shape, labels that are not u8/u16, a ref into
	// a segment that is not the overlay's, a malformed LUT, an inline field
	// over §9's bound -- or an `OverlayUpdate` sent to a chunked overlay, whose
	// unit of state is the chunk.  NOT a chunk the host did not ask for: that
	// is dropped and counted in the Plugin Manager's report, because a want
	// that left the set while the chunk was in flight is ordinary, not a
	// defect.  Its own site rather than 28 for the reason 29 has one: §12
	// aggregates per `where`, and a chunk defect and an item defect are two
	// counters.  Host -> plugin only.
	ViolationSite_VIOLATION_SITE_OVERLAY_CHUNK_REFUSED ViolationSite = 30
	// A processing stage found a STAGED slot's header invalid
	// (processing_stages.md §7): handed back `failed`, reason 1, and reported here.
	ViolationSite_VIOLATION_SITE_STAGE_SLOT_INVALID ViolationSite = 31
	// A StageArm the plugin cannot use: its segment_id names no installed "S"
	// segment, its stage_id no declared stage, or its pipeline or position is
	// out of range (processing_stages.md §4).  Not acknowledged; the
	// arrangement's deadline is how the host sees it.
	ViolationSite_VIOLATION_SITE_STAGE_ARM_REFUSED ViolationSite = 32
	// A `shape_proposal` or `shape_answer` out of step with the edits the host
	// began (control_plane_protocol.md §8.8): naming a `drag_id` never
	// sent to this session, a `seq` never sent within that drag, or answering
	// one `shape_commit` twice.  NOT a reply for a drag that has already ended,
	// which is dropped silently: the plugin cannot know the drag ended until
	// `shape_edit_ended` reaches it.  Host -> plugin only.
	ViolationSite_VIOLATION_SITE_SHAPE_OUT_OF_STEP ViolationSite = 33
	// A shape the host did not apply: a position count that is not the
	// region's vertex count, or a non-finite component.  A proposal leaves the
	// last shape on screen; an answer reverts the edit and `shape_edit_ended`
	// says so too.  Its own site rather than 33: that one is about WHEN a reply
	// came, this one about WHAT it said.  Host -> plugin only.
	ViolationSite_VIOLATION_SITE_SHAPE_REFUSED ViolationSite = 34
)

