// A plugin REQUESTS these in its manifest and the host assigns them here; the
// two are the same shape on purpose.
//
// A trigger ENGAGES a tool.  Once engaged, the tool owns the primary pointer in
// its views: the primary button means whatever the engaged tool means, so no
// tool ever names it -- a tool that wants a chord asks for a MOMENTARY trigger,
// which engages it for as long as the chord is held and hands the pointer back
// after.  A key may hold either way; a mouse chord is down or up and nothing
// else, so it is always momentary.
//
